C={x["paper_id"]:x for x in json.loads((D/"interspeech-2025-fortyfourth-d3-papers.json").read_text())["papers"]};P={x["paper_id"]:x for x in json.loads((D/"interspeech-2025-papers.json").read_text())["papers"]}
N={
"huang25d_interspeech":("A spoken dialogue tracker must use information across several turns, including references and corrections, rather than treating each utterance as a fresh request.","State is distributed over a conversation and graph relations change as entities are introduced, resolved, and revised.","Classify each turn independently or concatenate all text and hope a generic sequence model preserves the relevant relations.","Represent multi-turn spoken-language understanding with dynamic graph convolution so entities and dialogue relations update over time.","The paper models multi-turn spoken-language understanding with dynamic graph convolutional networks.","Dialogue state is a changing relational structure: the graph makes explicit which words, entities, and turns constrain the current interpretation.","The paper reports multi-turn spoken-language-understanding results for the dynamic graph model.","Domains, ASR errors, graph construction, turn length, labels, and evaluation split bound the result; a graph state is not complete conversational memory."),
"hutin25_interspeech":("A French schwa may be written as a distinct vowel, but listeners may not hear a stable acoustic difference from neighboring /ø/ and /œ/ in ordinary context.","Vowel categories are shaped by context, speaker, dialect, and lexical function; absence of an acoustic contrast does not mean absence of a linguistic role.","Measure average formants in isolated tokens and assume every phonological category has a separate acoustic target.","Compare schwa and its lexical neighbors in natural contexts while separating acoustic overlap from lexical and phonological distribution.","The paper argues that French schwa is not acoustically distinct from its two lexical neighbors /ø/ and /œ/.","The study separates category labels from acoustic contrast: a language can maintain a lexical distinction or alternation without a stable one-to-one formant separation.","The paper reports acoustic overlap between French schwa and the neighboring vowels in the tested materials.","Speakers, dialect, context, corpus, measurements, and lexical analysis bound the claim; acoustic overlap is not proof that all grammatical distinctions disappear."),
"jahan25_interspeech":("Speech technology should not work well only for the majority speakers or accents represented in its training data.","Fairness depends on which groups, tasks, and error costs are measured; one aggregate accuracy can hide unequal failures.","Report overall WER or accuracy and call the system fair, or compare groups without a reproducible benchmark.","Create a benchmark that measures fairness across speech-technology tasks and demographic or linguistic conditions with explicit group-level evidence.","FaiST is a benchmark dataset for fairness in speech technology.","Fairness becomes an evaluation object rather than a vague aspiration: group membership, task outcome, and error disparity must be linked while respecting privacy and sampling limits.","The paper reports benchmark resources and fairness evaluation results for the tested speech technologies.","Group definitions, labels, sample balance, tasks, metrics, and consent bound the conclusions; benchmark parity is not proof of social fairness."),
"johnson25_interspeech":("Annotating speech datasets is expensive, but letting an LLM help humans can introduce confident mistakes or erase disagreements that matter.","Speech labels depend on audio quality, context, dialect, and task definitions; an assistant can accelerate work only if people can inspect and correct its suggestions.","Accept LLM labels automatically or use humans without recording where model assistance changed a decision.","Build an exploratory human-in-the-loop framework for LLM-assisted speech annotation with review, uncertainty, and provenance.","The paper presents an exploratory framework for LLM-assisted human annotation of speech datasets.","Annotation is a coordination loop: the model proposes, the human adjudicates, and the system records evidence so speed does not replace accountability.","The paper reports framework behavior and exploratory annotation findings for the tested speech data tasks.","Task, annotator expertise, model, prompts, disagreement policy, and audit trail bound the result; assistance is not a substitute for label validity."),
"liu25p_interspeech":("A codec should restore missing high-frequency speech detail even when the input is noisy, without amplifying noise or making the speaker sound artificial.","The missing band is uncertain and noise can be mistaken for speech detail; a model must separate denoising from bandwidth expansion.","Copy a clean high band from a fixed template or extend bandwidth without modeling the noise condition.","Use a neural codec approach that jointly supports noise-robust bandwidth expansion and evaluates speech quality under noisy inputs.","The paper proposes a neural codec approach for noise-robust bandwidth expansion.","The codec treats missing frequency content and corruption as coupled inference: it must reconstruct a plausible high band conditioned on what the noisy low band actually supports.","The paper reports bandwidth-expansion quality and noise robustness for the proposed neural codec.","Noise types, bandwidth, codec rate, speakers, targets, and perceptual evaluation bound the result; plausible high-frequency detail is not ground truth."),
"lodagala25_interspeech":("Arabic TTS should handle standard, dialectal, and code-switched speech rather than presenting one standardized variety as all Arabic.","Pronunciation, vocabulary, rhythm, and switching behavior vary across varieties; data imbalance can make a model sound fluent only in the dominant subset.","Train on standard Arabic alone or concatenate dialect data without labeling the variety and switching context.","Build a benchmark corpus covering standard, dialectal, and code-switched Arabic TTS with documented speakers, text, and evaluation.","SawtArabi is a benchmark corpus for Arabic TTS spanning standard, dialectal, and code-switching speech.","Corpus design exposes variation as a modeling requirement: the system must preserve identity and naturalness while changing or mixing language variety.","The paper reports corpus resources and baseline TTS evaluations across the covered Arabic conditions.","Variety coverage, speaker balance, text design, switching labels, and listening tests bound generalization across Arabic communities."),
"mayer25_interspeech":("A speech synthesizer should vary prosody naturally across repeated generations while still following the intended text and style.","Prosody is multi-dimensional and uncertain; deterministic prediction can sound repetitive while unconstrained randomness can change emphasis or meaning.","Use one average prosody contour or inject random noise without controlling what it changes.","Investigate stochastic prosody-modeling methods and measure diversity together with intelligibility, naturalness, and controllability.","The paper investigates stochastic methods for prosody modeling in speech synthesis.","Prosody is a conditional distribution, not one correct curve: a useful model samples plausible timing and pitch while remaining anchored to linguistic content.","The paper reports stochastic prosody-modeling behavior and synthesis evaluations for the tested methods.","Text, speakers, sampling temperature, prosody labels, raters, and metrics bound the claim; diversity alone is not expressive control."),
"phukan25f_interspeech":("A child-safety system should detect harmful content in audiovisual material at a fine-grained level, using both what is said and what is shown.","Harm can be conveyed by words, objects, actions, or their combination; audio and vision arrive asynchronously and errors have different safety costs.","Classify the whole clip from audio or vision alone, or use one coarse harmful/not-harmful label.","Align audio and visual evidence with cascaded cross-transformers and predict fine-grained child-harm categories in real time.","SNIFR boosts fine-grained child harmful-content detection through audio-visual alignment with cascaded cross-transformers.","Safety understanding is multimodal grounding: the system must connect a specific spoken or visible event to a category and preserve timing for intervention.","The paper reports fine-grained audiovisual detection and real-time behavior for the tested harmful-content data.","Labels, age policy, modalities, cultures, false-positive costs, and latency bound the result; an automated detector is not a safeguarding decision-maker."),
}
